<p id="S3.F3.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(c)<span id="S3.F3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Laplacian editing</span></span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<div id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:72.5pt;"><img src="2510.02738v1/img/concept/laplacian_force_modulated_ee_traj.png" id="S3.F3.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:100/70;" width="100" height="70" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(d)<span id="S3.F3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Force-Laplacian</span></span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Example of trajectory warping with different modulation strategies. (a) is the demonstration trajectory, (b) shows the force-informed trajectory with virtual target, and (c) shows the trajectory warped by object and end effector initial pose, while (d) shows the complete trajectory we use in data generation warped both by object and end effector initial pose and force. </figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generating Force-Informed Data from Simulation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a light-weight data generation method for force-sensitive tasks without doing any training but directly warping a single human demonstration under new initial conditions in simulation. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, We first use FACTR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> with force feedback to teleoperate a Franka in IsaacGym and collect a human demonstration. We then split <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> into free-space and in-contact segments (Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> (length <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>) where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|F^{r}\|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|F^{r}\|=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> (length <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>) where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|F^{r}\|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|F^{r}\|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Prior to warping the demonstration trajectory, we apply domain randomization to the object’s pose, mass and friction in simulation, yielding new initial conditions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ee}^{r}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ee}^{r}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{obj}^{r}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{obj}^{r}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> that differ from those in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to generate a new demonstration consistent with these randomized conditions by decomposing and warping <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Free-space trajectory:</span> We warp the transition segment of the end-effector trajectory
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{ee}^{rf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{ee}^{rf}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> using Laplacian Editing (LE). LE allows directly modifying an existing trajectory defined by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> constrain waypoints <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> while capturing local properties. Let the free-space positions be <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ee}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ee}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,\ldots,T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,\ldots,T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. We anchor only two short bands:
the first <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{cs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{cs}</annotation></semantics></math> samples at the start and the last <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{ce}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ce}</annotation></semantics></math> samples at the end
(<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{cs}+n_{ce}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{cs}+n_{ce}=m</annotation></semantics></math>). The constraint simply preserves the <em id="S3.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative</em> offsets of these samples
to the new anchors: <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ee}^{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ee}^{new}</annotation></semantics></math> at the start and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{obj}^{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{obj}^{new}</annotation></semantics></math> at the end:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{ee,\mathrm{mod}}^{rf}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">mod</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{ee,\mathrm{mod}}^{rf}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{ee}^{new}+\big(p_{ee}(t)-p_{ee}(0)\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{ee}^{new}+\big(p_{ee}(t)-p_{ee}(0)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t=0{:}n_{cs}-1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t=0{:}n_{cs}-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{ee,\mathrm{mod}}^{rf}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">mod</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{ee,\mathrm{mod}}^{rf}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{obj}^{new}+\big(p_{ee}(t)-p_{ee}(T_{f})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{obj}^{new}+\big(p_{ee}(t)-p_{ee}(T_{f})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t=T_{f}-n_{ce}{:}T_{f}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t=T_{f}-n_{ce}{:}T_{f}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">That is, the first few points keep the same displacement from the new initial end-effector position, and the last few points keep the same displacement from the new object position. Then we first convert the constraint waypoints <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in Cartesian space into Laplacian coordinates <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> with the graph Laplacian matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\small L_{ij}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;i=j,\\[-2.0pt]
-\dfrac{w_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}w_{ij}},&amp;j\in\mathcal{N}_{i},\\[-2.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\small L_{ij}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;i=j,\\[-2.0pt]
-\dfrac{w_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}w_{ij}},&amp;j\in\mathcal{N}_{i},\\[-2.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are a set of neighbor points <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for waypoint <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight set to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for this work. One can obtain <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=L\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=L\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a concatenation of
the Laplacian coordinate for each waypoint</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\delta}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}\frac{w_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}w_{ij}}\,\bigl(\mathbf{r}_{i}-\mathbf{r}_{j}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\delta}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}\frac{w_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}w_{ij}}\,\bigl(\mathbf{r}_{i}-\mathbf{r}_{j}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> can be singular, so one can impose constraints on the system <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\mathbf{r}=\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\mathbf{r}=\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> when solving for new waypoints <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. To warp the rotational components of the free-space trajectory, we apply SLERP (Spherical Linear Interpolation of Rotations) between <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{ee}^{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{ee}^{new}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{obj}^{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{obj}^{new}</annotation></semantics></math>, interpolating the rotation part to the same length of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> which yields a smooth rotational transition over the entire free-space segment. Combining these orientations with the warped positions gives the complete modulated trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{ee_{mod}}^{rf}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}\times\mathcal{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{ee_{mod}}^{rf}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}\times\mathcal{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a)–(c), the object-pose modulation stretches the free-space path and smoothly reorients it in accordance with the object’s pose.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-contact trajectory:</span> For the in-contact part, we maintain an object-centric frame for trajectory warping. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{ee}^{rc},\mathbf{X}_{obj}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{ee}^{rc},\mathbf{X}_{obj}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the in-contact end-effector and object pose trajectory in the demonstration, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{obj}^{new}=[p_{obj}^{new},q_{obj}^{new}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{obj}^{new}=[p_{obj}^{new},q_{obj}^{new}]</annotation></semantics></math> the randomized initial object pose in simulation. For every data point, the warped end-effector pose <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)=[\mathbf{P}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i),\mathbf{Q}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)=[\mathbf{P}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i),\mathbf{Q}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)]</annotation></semantics></math> and force <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{warp}^{r}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{warp}^{r}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> are given by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{P}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{P}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{obj}^{new}+{\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}(i)}^{-1}(\mathbf{P}_{ee}^{rc}(i)-{\mathbf{P}_{obj}^{c}(i)})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{obj}^{new}+{\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}(i)}^{-1}(\mathbf{P}_{ee}^{rc}(i)-{\mathbf{P}_{obj}^{c}(i)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}(i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{obj}^{new}\,{\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}(i)}^{-1}\,\mathbf{Q}_{ee}^{rc}(i)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{obj}^{new}\,{\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}(i)}^{-1}\,\mathbf{Q}_{ee}^{rc}(i),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{warp}^{r}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{warp}^{r}(i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{obj}^{new}(i)\,\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}(i)^{-1}\,F^{r}(i)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{obj}^{new}(i)\,\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}(i)^{-1}\,F^{r}(i),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{obj}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{obj}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}_{obj}^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the positions and quaternions from the in-contact part object trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{obj}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{obj}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In III-A Generating Force-Informed Data from Simulation ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)(<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-A Generating Force-Informed Data from Simulation ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) preserves the relative end-effector pose in the object frame while Equation (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In III-A Generating Force-Informed Data from Simulation ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) rotates the demonstrated force into the randomized object frame. Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, which use an end effector velocity orthogonal to the contact surface to do force modulation, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, which steer toward a virtual target in the measured force direction, we construct a force-informed virtual target <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ee}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ee}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for each data point <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{ee_{w}}^{rc}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ee}^{v}=x_{ee_{w}}^{rc}+k_{f}F^{r}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ee}^{v}=x_{ee_{w}}^{rc}+k_{f}F^{r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hand-tuned parameter controlling the modulation strength. We claim that without the force-informed virtual target tracking, the task is not able to succeed because it has no intention of maintaining continuous contact. An analogy for the virtual target is a target pose inside the object. Moving to that target will generate force on the object surface. Comparing Figure <a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-C Compliant Policy Rollout with State Vector Field ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>(a) and <a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-C Compliant Policy Rollout with State Vector Field ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>(b), we could see that the force-informed trajectory gets into the object surface after adding the virtual target. In our method, the compliance scheduling term <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is supervised by force feedback: during policy learning, the contact force measured in simulation is converted to a damping target through a fixed linear mapping, and the flow-matching policy is trained to predict <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to match this target, which will be introduced in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Compliant Policy Rollout with State Vector Field ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compliant 3D Visuomotor Flow Matching Policy</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To learn visuomotor policies from simulation-generated data, we use a flow matching framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> as our core method. Flow matching runs at a higher frequency than diffusion-based models, making it suitable for contact-rich tasks. Prior work has shown that pointcloud inputs significantly enhance data efficiency and spatial generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Accordingly, we adopt a single-view point cloud as input, eliminating reliance on privileged object pose information. Thus, our observation space is defined as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=(o_{pc},o_{ee},o_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=(o_{pc},o_{ee},o_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use DP3 Encoder from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to encode the point cloud <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{pc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{pc}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also follow the same practice for processing the data, including furthest point sampling to 1024 points as well as cropping for the workspace. To be more contact-aware, we also include force <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> as part of the observation, which we use a simple 3-layer MLP to output a 64-D feature vector. The input also includes a feature vector encoded by a 3-layer MLP for the robot pose <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{ee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{ee}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then concatenate the three feature vectors as the observation condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The flow matching backbone in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, a conditional U-Net same as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, but with a different output space. In order to achieve compliant motion with contact in the environment, we augment the flow matching action space with a virtual pose trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{vt}^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{vt}^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math> and an impedance parameter trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the policy output is <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(X_{ref}^{t...t+H},X_{vt}^{t...t+H},d^{t...t+H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(X_{ref}^{t...t+H},X_{vt}^{t...t+H},d^{t...t+H})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are the robot end effector reference pose trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{ref}^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{ref}^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>, virtual pose trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{vt}^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{vt}^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as impedance parameter trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=16</annotation></semantics></math> as the policy output horizon in this work.
We will describe the reference pose, virtual pose, and the impedance parameter in detail in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Compliant Policy Rollout with State Vector Field ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>. Next, we formally define the flow matching component.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of conditional flow matching is to estimate a vector field <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\times[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\times[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that integrating the Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE)</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}z_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}\left(z_{t},t\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}z_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}\left(z_{t},t\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">on time <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> transports <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from base distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The base distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically chosen as Gaussian noise <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. To learn such a flow, training proceeds by matching <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> along an interpolation path <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=tz_{1}+(1-t)z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=tz_{1}+(1-t)z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. By taking the derivative, one will obtain the direction of a linear path <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}=z_{1}-z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}=z_{1}-z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the target velocity. We parametrize the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a conditional U-Net and matching the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the following regression problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>,</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\boldsymbol{v}}\mathbb{E}_{z_{0}\sim p_{0},z_{1}\sim p_{1}}\left[\int_{0}^{1}\left\|\boldsymbol{u}\left(z_{1},z_{0}\right)-\boldsymbol{v}_{\theta}\left(z_{t},t\right)\right\|_{2}^{2}\mathrm{~d}t\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝒗</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\boldsymbol{v}}\mathbb{E}_{z_{0}\sim p_{0},z_{1}\sim p_{1}}\left[\int_{0}^{1}\left\|\boldsymbol{u}\left(z_{1},z_{0}\right)-\boldsymbol{v}_{\theta}\left(z_{t},t\right)\right\|_{2}^{2}\mathrm{~d}t\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, the target sample <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the action in trajectory data <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{sim}</annotation></semantics></math> generated from the simulation. During inference, we first initiate a noisy action sample <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the ODE is integrated with Euler from <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on the observation <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> includes pointcloud <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{pc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{pc}</annotation></semantics></math>, end-effector pose <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{ee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{ee}</annotation></semantics></math>, and end-effector force <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t+\delta}=A_{t}+\delta\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}(A_{t},\mathcal{O})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t+\delta}=A_{t}+\delta\boldsymbol{v_{\theta}}(A_{t},\mathcal{O}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which we set the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for all of our experiments. After obtaining the actions rollout <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(X_{ref}^{t...t+H},X_{vt}^{t...t+H},d^{t...t+H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(X_{ref}^{t...t+H},X_{vt}^{t...t+H},d^{t...t+H})</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than tracking this trajectory with classical position-based impedance controller as commonly done in prior works, we introduce a different compliant rollout technique in the next section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compliant Policy Rollout with State Vector Field</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the policy rollout, we execute the resulting trajectory using a passive impedance controller in task space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. This controller ensures passive interaction with the environment, which is crucial when contact leads to constraints or sticking. Although the impedance parameter has been referenced as part of the policy action, we now formalize it. We begin with the control law from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{c}=G_{x}(x)-D(x)(\dot{x}-f(x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{c}=G_{x}(x)-D(x)(\dot{x}-f(x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the gravity compensation term (omitted from now on), <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the damping matrix, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the current end-effector velocity, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\dot{x}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\dot{x}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired velocity from the policy. The damping matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is structured via an eigendecomposition:
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)=V(x)\Lambda V(x)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)=V(x)\Lambda V(x)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix of damping gains and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x)=[v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x)=[v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthonormal matrix of eigenvectors, with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> aligned with the desired direction <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While damping-only impedance has the advantage of passivity, reducing damping sacrifices trajectory tracking, potentially causing distribution shifts. Moreover, tuning directional damping gains often requires significant manual effort and may degrade performance. To avoid these, we fix the damping magnitude and instead modulate the desired velocity direction to shape compliance. Unlike the flow matching vector field, this vector field is in the end effector task space during the execution stage. Specifically, we consider only the desired velocity component from Equation <a href="#S3.E11" title="In III-C Compliant Policy Rollout with State Vector Field ‣ III APPROACH ‣ Flow with the Force Field: Learning 3D Compliant Flow Matching Policies from Force and Demonstration-Guided Simulation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=D(x)f(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=D(x)f(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is collinear with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we can simplify to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}=v_{1}f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}=v_{1}f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We then decompose the desired velocity into magnitude and direction:
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=k\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=k\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired velocity magnitude, which can be adjusted online or designed, and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direction vector, which is your current desired moving direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{curr}\in R^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{curr}\in R^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the robot’s current end effector position, the rollout reference trajectory from flow matching be <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{ref}^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{ref}^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the virtual target trajectory be <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{vt}^{t...t+H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{vt}^{t...t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each reference <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{ref}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{ref}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has one corresponding virtual target <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{vt}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{vt}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{vt}^{t}-X_{ref}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{vt}^{t}-X_{ref}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> provides the compliant direction at <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{ref}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{ref}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we now define <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}=X_{ref}^{t+1}-X_{ref}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}=X_{ref}^{t+1}-X_{ref}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the compliant direction <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{n}=X_{vt}^{t}-x_{curr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{n}=X_{vt}^{t}-x_{curr}</annotation></semantics></math>. In ideal case, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{curr}\approx X_{ref}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{curr}\approx X_{ref}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We construct <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math> to blend between the reference motion direction <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and compliant direction <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}=\frac{d\hat{n}+\hat{t}}{||d\hat{n}+\hat{t}||}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}=\frac{d\hat{n}+\hat{t}}{||d\hat{n}+\hat{t}||},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a gain controlling convergence toward the virtual target and the degree of compliance. This gain is the impedance parameter of our flow-matching policy. During training, the gain <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is scheduled based on the estimated force magnitude <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}|</annotation></semantics></math> collected by the force sensor in the simulation. Similar to the stiffness in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. We define it as:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E14.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:258.8pt;height:56.2pt;vertical-align:-24.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(51.1pt,-11.1pt) scale(1.65276451976921,1.65276451976921) ;">
<p id="S3.E14.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E14.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\begin{cases}d_{\uparrow},&amp;|\mathcal{F}|&lt;\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}\\
d{\uparrow}-(d_{\uparrow}-d_{\downarrow})\frac{|\mathcal{F}|-\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}}{\mathcal{F}{\uparrow}-\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}},&amp;\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}\leq|\mathcal{F}|\leq\mathcal{F}{\uparrow}\\
d{\downarrow},&amp;|\mathcal{F}|&gt;\mathcal{F}{\uparrow}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\begin{cases}d_{\uparrow},&amp;|\mathcal{F}|&lt;\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}\\
d{\uparrow}-(d_{\uparrow}-d_{\downarrow})\frac{|\mathcal{F}|-\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}}{\mathcal{F}{\uparrow}-\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}},&amp;\mathcal{F}{\downarrow}\leq|\mathcal{F}|\leq\mathcal{F}{\uparrow}\\
d{\downarrow},&amp;|\mathcal{F}|&gt;\mathcal{F}{\uparrow}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\uparrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\uparrow}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\downarrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\downarrow}</annotation></semantics></math> are hardware-specific maximum and minimum values. This formulation enables directional compliance to emerge naturally from modulating <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, without sacrificing tracking fidelity. While this formulation might break passivity with such usage, we argue that this state-dependent vector field rollout will allow safer interruption during execution when interacting with the environment. Furthermore, we will show in the experiments that this formulation actually offers better performance in the real world contact tasks while at the same time injecting less energy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
